      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the prime factorization of 400.\n2. Determine the sum of the positive divisors of 400.\n3. Find the distinct prime factors of the sum of the divisors.\n\nLet's start with the prime factorization of 400:\n\\[ 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nThe formula for the sum of the divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\n\nFor \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) \\]\n\\[ = (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) \\]\n\\[ = 31 \\times 31 = 31^2 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the distinct prime factors of 31. Since 31 is a prime number, its only distinct prime factor is 31.\n\nSo, the sum of the positive divisors of 400 has \\(\\boxed{1}\\) distinct prime factor.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to confirm our result.\n\n```python\nimport sympy\n\n# Function to calculate the sum of divisors\ndef sum_of_divisors(n):\n    return sympy.divisor_sigma(n, [1])\n\n# Calculate the sum of divisors of 400\nsum_divisors = sum_of_divisors(400)\n\n# Find the distinct prime factors of the sum of divisors\nprime_factors = sympy.primefactors(sum_divisors)\n\n# Number of distinct prime factors\nnum_prime_factors = len(prime_factors)\n\n# Output the result\nprint(num_prime_factors)\n```",
