      "text": "To determine the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\), we first need to find the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the sum of the divisors of a number. If a number \\(n\\) has the prime factorization \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\), the sum of the divisors of \\(n\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)\n\\]\n\nWe calculate each term separately:\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the prime factorization of \\(961\\). We check if \\(961\\) is a perfect square:\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(961\\) is \\(31^2\\). The distinct prime factor of \\(961\\) is \\(31\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
